<ManualLayout slug="ai">
  <h2 id="on-device">On-device by default</h2>
  <p>
    The intelligence features use <strong>Apple Intelligence</strong>, running on your Mac. Your
    notes are processed locally and are not sent to us or to any third party. These features appear
    only on hardware where Apple Intelligence is available and enabled in System Settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything else in HelloNotes works without them — the AI is an addition, never a requirement.
  </p>

  <h2 id="note-actions">Note actions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Summarise</strong> — a short summary of the current note.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggest tags</strong> — proposes tags based on the content; you choose which to keep.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggest links</strong> — finds notes that are plausibly related and offers to link them.</li>
    <li><strong>Expand</strong> — fleshes out a stub or bullet into prose.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rewrite selection</strong> — improve, shorten, simplify or restructure the selected
      text. You see the result and choose <em>Replace</em> or <em>Insert below</em>; nothing changes
      until you do.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ask">Ask your library</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ask Library</strong> answers questions using your own notes as the source. It finds the
    relevant ones, reads them, and answers with <strong>citations</strong> back to the notes it drew
    on so you can check the reasoning and jump to the original.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is most useful for questions you know you have written about but cannot place — “what did I
    decide about the pricing model?” rather than general knowledge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="assistant">The Assistant</h2>
  <p>
    The Assistant is a chat window with tools: it can search your collection, read notes, propose
    edits, and — if you enable them — search and fetch from the web.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>Edits always require your approval.</strong> When the Assistant wants to change a
      note, it shows you the exact change first and waits. Nothing is written to disk until you
      approve it.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>Skills</strong> let you save reusable instructions for repeated jobs, and
    <strong>deep research</strong> runs a longer multi-step investigation across your notes and,
    optionally, the web.
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers">Using your own model</h2>
  <p>
    If you would rather not use Apple Intelligence — or your Mac does not support it — you can
    configure a different model in <strong>Settings ▸ AI</strong>.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Local</strong>, nothing leaves your machine:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ollama</strong> — run <code>ollama serve</code>; no key needed.</li>
    <li><strong>LM Studio</strong> — start its local server; no key needed.</li>
    <li><strong>MLX</strong> — runs a downloaded model on Apple silicon.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Cloud</strong>, using your own API key:</p>
  <p>
    Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, Mistral, Groq, OpenRouter, xAI, DeepSeek, Cerebras, Together AI,
    Perplexity and Ollama Cloud are supported. Paste your key in Settings; it is stored in the macOS
    Keychain and never sent to us.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Cloud providers are <strong>off until you configure one</strong>. When you use one, the text
      for that action goes to that provider under your own account and their privacy policy — see
      the <a href={href('/privacy')}>privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="settings">Choosing where each feature runs</h2>
  <p>
    Settings ▸ AI has two separate choices: the <strong>chat provider</strong> (Assistant and Ask
    Library) and the <strong>intelligence provider</strong> (Summarise, Suggest, Expand). Both
    default to on-device Apple Intelligence. <strong>Creativity</strong> controls how varied the
    output is — lower is more focused and predictable.
  </p>
</ManualLayout>
